@node Keys in the goal display
@section Keys in the goal display

Goals arrive as trees in which every subterm is labelled, so point can
rest on any part of a goal and ask about it.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{RET}
@tab Press the control at point, or go to a message's position
@tab @code{lean4-info-return}
@item @samp{M-.}
@tab Jump to the definition of the subterm at point
@tab @code{xref-find-definitions}
@item @samp{C-c C-t}
@tab Jump to the definition of its type
@tab @code{lean4-info-goto-type-definition}
@item @samp{TAB}
@tab Fold or unfold the trace or section at point
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-fold}
@item @samp{C-c C-s}
@tab Pin this position, or unpin the section point is in
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pin}
@item @samp{C-c C-SPC}
@tab Pause or unpause the display
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pause}
@item @samp{C-c C-g}
@tab Bring a paused display up to date
@tab @code{lean4-info-refresh-paused}
@item @samp{C-c C-a}
@tab Pause or unpause the file's message list
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-all-messages-pause}
@item @samp{C-c C-o}
@tab Order that list by position or by nearness
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-message-order}
@end multitable

Pinning a position keeps it on display as a section of its own, above
the section following point, and any number of positions can be pinned.
Each keeps updating: a pin is a marker, so it follows its declaration as
the file is edited, and it stays on display after moving to another
file.  Each pinned section has its own controls -- one back to the position,
one to pause it, one to bring it up to date while paused, and one to
unpin it.  Pausing a pin is separate from pinning it: a pinned goal
follows its declaration through an edit unless told otherwise.  Pinning happens at point, so the followed
section is left out until point moves, rather than showing the same
position twice; returning to a pinned position later does show both.

Every control has a key and a menu entry as well, so none of them needs
a mouse.  The menu entries are the same in the Lean menu and in the goal
display's own, since every one of these commands works from either
buffer; each says what pressing it will do next, so an item reads
"Unpin this position" where the position is already pinned.
With several pins there is no one pin for a key to mean, so
@samp{C-c C-s} means the section point is standing in: inside a pinned
section it unpins that one, and anywhere else it pins or unpins the
position point is on in the Lean buffer.  @samp{C-c C-SPC} and @samp{C-c C-g} read the same way: inside a
pinned section they pause and refresh that section, anywhere else the
one following point.  @samp{RET} reaches the two things in the display that
lead anywhere: on a control it does what clicking it would, and on a
message heading it goes to the place the message was reported for.
Every control is therefore reachable without a mouse.

The last two are bound here as well as in the Lean buffer, so the
display can be pinned or paused without leaving it.  Clicking their
controls does the same, and leaves the keyboard in the Lean buffer
rather than taking focus, as VS Code does.  Both states can hold at
once, and the heading says so: pinned means the display has stopped
following point, paused that it has stopped updating at all.

Where there is nothing at point to report -- outside a proof, say -- the
display says @samp{No info found.} rather than going blank, which would read
like a display that had stopped working.  Messages from elsewhere in the
file are still listed below it.

The type and docstring of the subterm at point are reported through
ElDoc, so they appear wherever ElDoc is configured to put them: the
echo area by default, or a child frame with a package such as
@samp{eldoc-box}.

Traces fold like everything else, being sections themselves: @samp{TAB} or
the mouse, with the same indicator.  Lean does not send the children of
a trace node until something asks -- a @samp{simp} trace on a real proof is
enormous -- so opening one fetches them, once.

@node The tactic state
@section The tactic state

The goals at point are listed under @samp{Tactic state}, with the number of
them on the line below, both as VS Code words and places them.  A single
goal is counted too -- @samp{1 goal} -- so the line is there on every proof
rather than only on a branching one.

A finished proof reads @samp{goals accomplished} instead, which is Lean's own
wording for it; VS Code says @samp{No goals} there and counts nothing, and
neither does this.

The expected type at point follows, under @samp{Expected type}, and is not
counted -- VS Code counts only the tactic state.  Neither heading takes
a colon, as neither does in VS Code.

@node Copying what is on display
@section Copying what is on display

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Copies
@item @code{lean4-info-copy-state}
@tab the tactic state, to the kill ring
@item @code{lean4-info-copy-to-comment}
@tab the tactic state, into the Lean buffer
@item @code{lean4-info-copy-message}
@tab the message point is in, to the kill ring
@end multitable

The first two are in both menus and work from either buffer; the third
needs point to be inside a message in the goal display, messages being
sections.  @code{lean4-info-copy-to-comment} puts the state in a @samp{/- -/} block
above the line point is on, indented with it, as VS Code's "Copy Contents
to Comment" does.

The state is copied in full, whatever the settings above are hiding: text
pasted into an issue is no use with half the hypotheses missing.  This is
what VS Code copies too.

@node How much of a goal to show
@section How much of a goal to show

A goal in Mathlib can carry more hypotheses than fit on a screen, most of
them typeclass instances nobody is reasoning about.  Eight settings decide
how much of one is shown, the same eight VS Code gathers behind the cog in
its InfoView.  They are in the @samp{Lean} menu and the goal display's own,
under @samp{How much of a goal to show}, as checkboxes; each is also a command
and an option.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Setting
@tab Default
@tab Command
@item @code{lean4-info-show-goal-names}
@tab @samp{t}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-goal-names}
@item @code{lean4-info-emphasize-first-goal}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-emphasize-first-goal}
@item @code{lean4-info-target-first}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-target-first}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-type-assumptions}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-type-assumptions}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-instance-assumptions}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-instance-assumptions}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-inaccessible-assumptions}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-inaccessible-assumptions}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-let-values}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-let-values}
@item @code{lean4-info-expected-type-visibility}
@tab @samp{expanded}
@tab @code{lean4-info-cycle-expected-type}
@end multitable

Every default is VS Code's, so out of the box the whole goal is shown.
The commands change a setting for the session; customizing it makes the
change stick.

@itemize
@item
Goal names are the @samp{case inl} labels a structured proof produces.
@item
Emphasizing the first goal draws the others smaller and dimmer -- the
main goal is the one being worked on, the rest are context.
@item
Showing the target first puts @samp{⊢} above the hypotheses, and reverses
them, so that the target stays next to the hypothesis it was next to.
@item
Type assumptions are hypotheses that are themselves types, such as the
implicit @samp{α : Type} of a general lemma.  Instance assumptions are
typeclass instances, @samp{[Inhabited α]} and its kind.  Hiding either drops
the hypothesis entirely.
@item
Inaccessible assumptions are the ones Lean prints with a dagger, @samp{h✝},
which cannot be referred to by name.  They are dimmed rather than
hidden by default.  Where a dagger name shares a line with a usable
one, hiding takes away just the name.
@item
Hiding let-values shows a @samp{let} binder's type but not its value, which
on a large value is the difference between reading the goal and
scrolling past it.
@item
The expected type can be shown, shown folded, or left out.  The middle
two are defaults rather than rules: the section still folds and unfolds
with @samp{TAB}, and a fold made by hand survives until the setting changes.
@end itemize

Goals are kept as Lean sent them and rendered on the way into the
buffer, so changing one of these costs a redraw rather than another
request.  That is what lets a change reach a pinned or paused section,
where re-fetching would be precisely the wrong thing.  Goals from a
server too old for the interactive RPC arrive as text and cannot be
filtered; the settings simply have nothing to act on.

@node Errors and warnings
@section Errors and warnings

Diagnostics are reported through Flymake, which Eglot drives.  @samp{C-c ! n}
and @samp{C-c ! p} move between them, and @code{flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics}
lists them.

Not everything Lean sends is a problem.  It reports a completed proof,
trace output and whatever a project's own tactics emit at note severity,
all of which is meant for the goal display; flagging it in the editor
buries the errors among it.  So Flymake is shown errors and warnings,
and the rest goes to the goal display only.  Set
@code{lean4-flymake-minimum-severity} to @samp{note} to be shown everything, as VS
Code's Problems view does, or to @samp{error} for less.

Messages appear in the goal display placed by Lean's own notion of
which declaration they belong to, so a message about a multi-line
declaration stays visible while point moves through it.  Terms inside a
message are as interactive as terms inside a goal.

Those covering point are listed under @samp{Messages}, set in under the
heading naming the position, along with the goals and the expected type.
Every message in the file is listed under @samp{All messages}, at the outer
level, with two controls of its own: one orders the list by nearness to point or by
position in the file -- nearness first by default, as in VS Code, whose
manual has All Messages "sorted by their proximity to the text cursor by
default" -- and one holds the list as it is, separately from the pause in
the position heading.  While the display is paused a third control
appears in the position heading, between the way back to the pinned
position and the pin, which brings it up to date without unpausing it.  Both headings count what is in them by severity, the way VS
Code's do -- @samp{All messages (1 ⊗  5 ⓘ)} for a file with one error and
five notes.

Messages Lean marks as being for the goal display rather than the
editor -- that is what @samp{isSilent} means, the completed-proof report
among them -- are shown against the position they belong to and kept out
of both Flymake and @samp{All messages}, as VS Code does with them.

Lean emits three severities: error @samp{⊗}, warning @samp{⚠} and information
@samp{ⓘ}.  LSP defines a fourth, hint, which this package draws as @samp{ⓗ}, but
Lean's own @code{MessageSeverity} has no such value and its server never
sends one.  The glyphs are picked for the frame, falling back to @samp{E W I
H} where none can be displayed.

Each message is a section of its own, so a long one -- a @samp{simp} trace, a
mismatch between two large terms -- folds away without folding the rest.
Sections are set in with spaces and their fold indicators drawn by
@code{magit-section}, in the fringe, the way a Magit buffer does both.
Its heading names the file, line and column, and carries an @samp{↪} control,
right-aligned, that sends point there.  Clicking works as @samp{mouse-1} does
throughout Emacs: on a control it acts, and anywhere else it moves
point -- which is how a subterm is put under ElDoc, and how VS Code
reads a plain click too.  @samp{RET} does the same two things by keyboard,
going to the message's position from anywhere on its heading; the
definition of a subterm is @samp{M-.}, wherever your xref frontend puts it.
Folding is @samp{TAB}, and by mouse whatever
@code{magit-section} itself offers -- a double click, or a click on the
fringe or margin, from version 4; nothing in 3.3.0 -- so this display
folds the way your Magit does.  The same control appears in the
top heading, but only while pinned: unpinned, the display is following
point and it would only lead where point already is.

@node Building dependencies
@section Building dependencies

Visiting a file does not build what it imports.  The server decides that
from a field on the notification announcing the file, and @samp{C-c C-d}
(@code{lean4-refresh-file-dependencies}) is what asks for a build: it closes
and reopens the file, telling Lean to build a dependency that is out of
date on the way.  That is the command to reach for after editing an
import, or when the goal display reports an import that cannot be found.
VS Code calls it "Server: Restart File".

The build happens once.  If the file worker then crashes, reopening the
file does not set the build going again -- a distinction worth having on a
file whose imports do not compile, where the alternative is a rebuild per
crash.

Set @code{lean4-automatically-build-dependencies} to @samp{t} to build on every
open instead, as VS Code's @code{lean4.automaticallyBuildDependencies} does.
Both default off for the same reason: on a project the size of Mathlib,
opening one file to read it should not commit you to building everything
beneath it.  @samp{C-c C-p C-l} (@code{lean4-lake-build}) builds the package
outright.

@node Building
@section Building

@samp{C-c C-p C-l} (@code{lean4-lake-build}) builds the package the way VS Code's
"Build Project" does, which is three steps rather than a bare @samp{lake
build}:

@enumerate
@item
@samp{lake resolve-deps} clones a dependency the manifest names but the
checkout has not got.
@item
@samp{lake exe cache get} downloads Mathlib's build artefacts, where that
executable exists -- see @ref{Mathlib's build cache}.  On a project that
depends on Mathlib this is the step between minutes and hours.
@item
@samp{lake build} builds what is left.
@end enumerate

Each step is conditional on the one before, so a failure stops the rest,
and all three report into one compilation buffer where @samp{M-x next-error}
walks whatever any of them said.

The cache step is skipped, without complaint, in a project that has no
@samp{cache} executable -- most projects.  A cache download that @emph{fails},
though, stops the build rather than leaving it to do that work locally.

For a bare @samp{lake build}, with no network and no dependency resolution,
@samp{M-x compile} is the thing.

@node Mathlib's build cache
@section Mathlib's build cache

Mathlib is far too large to build locally, and does not need to be: its
artifacts are built upstream and can be downloaded.  Three commands ask
for them, all through @samp{lake exe cache get}, which is Mathlib's own
executable rather than anything Lake provides -- so in a project that
does not depend on Mathlib they fail, and Lake says so in the
compilation buffer.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{C-c C-p C-c}
@tab The whole package
@tab @code{lean4-lake-fetch-cache}
@item @samp{C-c C-p C-f}
@tab This file and what it imports
@tab @code{lean4-lake-fetch-file-cache}
@item 
@tab Every open Lean file in the package
@tab @code{lean4-lake-fetch-open-file-caches}
@end multitable

The last is in the Lean menu, under @samp{Project}, along with the other two
and @samp{lake build}.  Fetching for one file is much quicker than for the
whole of Mathlib and is usually what is wanted; fetching for the package
is the thing to run once, after cloning.

Output goes to a compilation buffer, so @samp{M-x next-error} walks whatever
Lake reports, as with any other build.

@node The module hierarchy
@section The module hierarchy

What a module imports, and what imports it, as a tree in a
@samp{*Lean Module Hierarchy*} buffer.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Shows
@item @code{lean4-module-hierarchy}
@tab what this file's module imports
@item @code{lean4-module-hierarchy-inverse}
@tab what imports this file's module
@item @code{lean4-module-copy-name}
@tab copies the module's name
@end multitable

All three are in the @samp{Lean} menu under @samp{Module hierarchy}.  VS Code binds
its two to @samp{Alt+Shift+M} and @samp{Alt+Shift+N}; no keys are bound here, those
chords not being Emacs ones, so bind them to taste:

@lisp
(keymap-set lean4-mode-map "C-c C-p C-m" #'lean4-module-hierarchy)
@end lisp

The buffer is a @code{magit-section} tree, so it folds with @samp{TAB} and moves the
way the goal display does.  @samp{RET} visits the module at point, @samp{g} asks for
the tree again, and @samp{q} buries it.

A level is fetched when it is opened, not before: Mathlib's import graph
is far too large to ask for at once.  An unopened node shows @samp{...} until
its children arrive.  A module that already appears further up the tree is
marked @samp{(shown above)} and not expanded, since the import graph has
diamonds and following one would go round for ever.

Imports of a module using Lean's @samp{module} keyword carry modifiers, and
those are shown beside the name: @samp{private}, @samp{all}, @samp{meta}, or @samp{meta and
non-meta} for one imported both ways.

@menu
* It needs a built project::
@end menu

@node It needs a built project
@subsection It needs a built project

The server answers these from its reference data, which it builds from the
@samp{.ilean} files a build leaves behind.  In a project that has not been
built the tree is empty -- correctly so, there being nothing indexed to
report.  Run @samp{C-c C-p C-l} (@code{lean4-lake-build}), or
@samp{C-c C-p C-c} (@code{lean4-lake-fetch-cache}) on a Mathlib project, first.

@code{lean4-enable-file-watchers} is off by default, so a tree fetched after a
rebuild may be the one from before it; @samp{g} asks again.

@node Other Lake operations
@section Other Lake operations

Two more commands live in the same @samp{Project} menu.

@code{lean4-lake-clean} runs @samp{lake clean}, and asks first: on a project the
size of Mathlib that discards hours of building, though
@code{lean4-lake-fetch-cache} gets most of it back.

@code{lean4-lake-update-dependency} runs @samp{lake update} on one dependency,
offering the package's direct Git dependencies for completion -- read
from @samp{lake-manifest.json}, which is Lake's record of what it resolved
them to.  It asks first too, and means it: VS Code's warning is that the
command is for maintainers of a project, and that if the new version is
incompatible with another dependency or with the code here, the project
may stop building.  It also rewrites the manifest, which most projects
keep under version control.

Unlike VS Code it leaves @samp{lean-toolchain} alone.  VS Code offers to match
it to the updated dependency's; that is a second decision, about a file
both Lake and elan read, and is better made deliberately.

Opening a project needs nothing from this package: a Lean file is served
by whichever workspace encloses it, so @samp{C-x C-f} and @samp{project.el} are the
whole of it.  VS Code's "Open Local Project" exists because it has a
window to point at a folder.

@node Completion
@section Completion

Completion is offered through @code{completion-at-point-functions}, so any
completion interface works: the default @samp{*Completions*} buffer,
@samp{company}, @samp{corfu} or anything else.  Three sources are active in a Lean
buffer.  Eglot's is one.  Another is for the Unicode abbreviations, so
that typing @samp{\al} offers @samp{\alpha} and inserting it produces α.  The
third is for Lean's keywords, because the server's own completion is
contextual and offers nothing towards a bare word at the top level:
@samp{exam} gets no help towards @samp{example} from Lean, and VS Code fills the
same gap with editor-level word suggestions.

None of the three is exclusive, Eglot's included -- its entry is
rewrapped to say so.  Left as Eglot leaves it, a prefix the server had
nothing for ended completion there instead of falling through to
whatever else you have configured.

Typing an abbreviation in full still works without completion: the Lean
input method replaces it as soon as it is unambiguous.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

Lean4-Mode uses @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/eglot/, Eglot}, the LSP client built into Emacs 29 and later, so
Eglot's own configuration applies.

